#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3ee74da8-3a3b-5119-b34f-aee3f5770302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21832" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t).$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\sinh(3t) = \\dfrac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+3)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty),$ we have that $\\mathcal L\\{\\sinh(3t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty) \\cap (-3,\\infty) = (3,\\infty),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147296 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "c012d177-e70b-5332-b5b2-91bf9b829eca" :question/math-academy-id "e-6647" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-3t}-e^{5t}$ for $s>5.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{5t}} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}+L{-{e}^{5t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3 \\\\ L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{5t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-5)-(s+3)}{(s+3)(s-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{(s+3)(s-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{(s+3)(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{5t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty),$ we have that $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty) \\cap (5,\\infty) = (5,\\infty),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147321 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "db1ffc3b-835c-5b53-bd41-802a7841e3ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-6736" :question/problem "Given that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 3\\cos{9t} - 2\\sin{9t}$ for $s > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t} & =L{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t}+L{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t} \\\\  & =3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t} & =\\frac{9}{{s}^{2}+81},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{{s}^{2}+81} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+81}-\\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+81} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s-18}{{s}^{2}+81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "671ba222-548d-574c-8db1-826341a73a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671ba222-548d-574c-8db1-826341a73a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319999} :answer-field/id #uuid "2acd52f2-66ea-54c2-a7c0-7f7e5846291c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c168ff21-8a4c-5e86-adf9-7cc64b86bf09" :question/math-academy-id "q-331458" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}$ for $s>5.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}} & =L{{e}^{5t}}+L{-{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-2)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s-2-s+5}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-5)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1067878-c74f-5fb2-8483-b56a92a0aba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1067878-c74f-5fb2-8483-b56a92a0aba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320002} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b607c5-ad2f-5abc-a187-f40febd6fc45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b015829-58ae-5e70-bc83-8b1a18798403" :question/math-academy-id "q-331459" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}+L{-{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3 \\\\ L{{e}^{t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,s>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-1)-(s+3)}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(s+3)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0175c04-e4da-5fc4-ba40-43c65c540cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0175c04-e4da-5fc4-ba40-43c65c540cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320005} :answer-field/id #uuid "0791a5d5-3615-5b0a-a2ec-01635434cc40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0460a9f5-0665-594f-936d-19b393b30df9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331463" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3-5{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bce9b3f-540e-5c17-978c-a8a464becb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320008} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d330e7-fdf3-56fa-b63a-d51e5d96ea23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bce9b3f-540e-5c17-978c-a8a464becb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320008} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3a05bb-e570-55f2-b1bc-51b9a4ea3f4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7560d20f-6a91-5fd6-b240-b4b3e7d11194" :question/math-academy-id "q-331464" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=1-3{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1-3{e}^{4t}} & =L{1}+L{-3{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-3s}{s(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+2)}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6b398f-1d6d-5cd3-8273-1e732c19abe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5s+8}{s^2+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320012} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ce8b1-69d4-5a5e-a298-51c646117a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5s-8}{s^2+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ce8b1-69d4-5a5e-a298-51c646117a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5s-8}{s^2+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b91c37c-78fe-5d7c-8d7e-ffd7f7a6e2fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f6b30aa-5797-5213-a5e2-927d71d7ab28" :question/math-academy-id "q-331500" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}+L{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} \\\\  & =5L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s-8}{{s}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea291f3-25c0-51c1-9dfb-d72431da31df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7s+12}{s^2+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea291f3-25c0-51c1-9dfb-d72431da31df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7s+12}{s^2+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320016} :answer-field/id #uuid "03cdff83-a39d-55d5-857f-05eb3b5260cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bce0c66-363d-500f-a770-0628ad5332cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-331504" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =L{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+L{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =7L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+4L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =7L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+4L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7s+12}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b323ec43-0827-5b71-8859-662f90d2632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > − 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144308} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e15efb-d691-54d8-aa1e-c5ddbd466ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s ≥ 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144309} {:answer/id #uuid "faa2bf73-374d-5c2d-a6e0-ef5033994ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s ≥ − 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144310} {:answer/id #uuid "07e547cd-3159-51ca-a62d-592e956c2862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144311} {:answer/id #uuid "26be6651-a87f-5890-a3fd-fe2f50565750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144312} {:answer/id #uuid "611c9868-3557-5e2a-9421-cb597e13e6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s ≥ 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07e547cd-3159-51ca-a62d-592e956c2862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1da1ced-cfcb-5a7e-b092-a490ca7f133b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3d42b8-7bca-5e16-a8bd-5f9681d2ed24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(s)/(s^{2} − 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144315} {:answer/id #uuid "fa37942b-5ff7-5210-a958-62f6acd583e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(s^{2} + 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144316} {:answer/id #uuid "fa88d130-5adb-5884-ba32-19178cc20e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(s)/(s^{2} + 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144317} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3c33a8-9e2c-5aaa-904d-edc348bf0fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(s^{2} − 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144318} {:answer/id #uuid "50692920-a866-53a1-928d-1d40dcc295a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3s)/(s^{2} + 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa37942b-5ff7-5210-a958-62f6acd583e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(s^{2} + 9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144316} :answer-field/id #uuid "255d167c-9b57-5056-ad04-e4ff0d68db47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4749b4-7bc7-5f25-b6f4-236f9d3ed717" :question/math-academy-id "q-340665" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the sine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}-\\frac{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{(s+3\\text{i})-(s-3\\text{i})}{(s-3\\text{i})(s+3\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{{s}^{2}+9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674c91be-90d5-5397-b9e8-57b4312eaf74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320019} {:answer/id #uuid "eec7a445-04d7-593d-9f35-38153e563fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320020} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e7ceb7-c1d5-5f9d-b98d-00e9282bf247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320021} {:answer/id #uuid "93426891-5868-5b0a-b768-f920caae587e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320022} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6425ad-42a4-50c4-8dbb-e632e407f2bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec7a445-04d7-593d-9f35-38153e563fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320020} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7349208-8baa-5614-b6ba-6f2ee34a2084" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614b8e57-7cfc-52e1-87fb-2720166201e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320025} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ee42cd-823a-59bd-81fd-ecb41ff18ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320026} {:answer/id #uuid "96e63ae1-3fcb-52ba-a255-0a71bc609812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320027} {:answer/id #uuid "f21439fa-7086-52f5-86c1-25e9e3d2aad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320028} {:answer/id #uuid "66f1d5b8-87be-5fa5-92c9-7f941c82e89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320029} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3640bf-7547-55e2-b620-fce07a818478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614b8e57-7cfc-52e1-87fb-2720166201e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320025} :answer-field/id #uuid "56031e02-a48c-5b8c-a484-21e2037b38cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76cf24a0-9805-5350-bd6b-b9aff0267017" :question/math-academy-id "q-341107" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+2)+(s-2)}{(s-2)(s+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a6d38d-800c-5224-a21c-b9070f907767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320033} {:answer/id #uuid "92bcfa22-5788-585a-8ee7-e14f46a91c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320034} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8d0c1e-b1b4-50ea-9796-bbefbbcb852a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320035} {:answer/id #uuid "67cff539-a3b9-5d72-9d5a-ef108c5d5463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320036} {:answer/id #uuid "8f890622-6fe9-51ca-bbb0-75f278dc4a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92bcfa22-5788-585a-8ee7-e14f46a91c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320034} :answer-field/id #uuid "2253fc46-c22a-54ab-b498-6ced5b22d68a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292c8481-0f5a-515b-b8b9-7401829500a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320039} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a58c51-5ac5-5324-877a-31d8451ca52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320040} {:answer/id #uuid "c0898d29-d9c3-5793-aa87-830824ae2a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320041} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0a78b3-8824-5702-8be4-655150ec42cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320042} {:answer/id #uuid "06173a57-b1d0-525d-ab3d-44059927cf60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320043} {:answer/id #uuid "3c04c114-e641-5762-8cd0-b9fb5a400617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "292c8481-0f5a-515b-b8b9-7401829500a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320039} :answer-field/id #uuid "242e250f-971a-56fd-92a2-c9094eb117f4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1463b6de-f11a-5f9e-b1d5-6cc54aaacf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-341109" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4},\\,s>-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4)+(s-4)}{(s-4)(s+4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78627af5-1bf5-527a-a740-e124f37b8ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}-144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320047} {:answer/id #uuid "8a001fa0-bd54-53ff-8b8c-7c0a7a8f67ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12s}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320048} {:answer/id #uuid "68a74458-1174-567d-99e3-ab6ef44838e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320049} {:answer/id #uuid "f40ed9b6-feca-56b6-876b-1056bfedd1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320050} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d3603e-7225-5597-826b-a3e69d083d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a74458-1174-567d-99e3-ab6ef44838e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320049} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0f1d1b-73ae-588e-96ae-a02e338d5afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f006d4-b85a-56c9-88e8-ed36d3199fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320053} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7c7321-a689-579d-a9e3-00e9cd157d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320054} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fea182-73e7-578b-91db-62b453c6aee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320055} {:answer/id #uuid "bc84cf58-2fdf-54e1-bc65-7df3dd9c33d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320056} {:answer/id #uuid "396923ad-310e-5531-a433-ae8cbdbc1afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320057} {:answer/id #uuid "ac62991a-fb0c-5116-b7ca-34f08321c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac62991a-fb0c-5116-b7ca-34f08321c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320058} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa990a5a-0ead-5f5b-97af-cabc30bd9a5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d5b00b2-0711-5fe4-a60e-34aa63f78154" :question/math-academy-id "q-341127" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the sine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)=\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}-\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)} & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{(s+12\\text{i})-(s-12\\text{i})}{(s-12\\text{i})(s+12\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{24\\text{i}}{{s}^{2}+144}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+144},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ccb86bb-2fa0-5770-a460-6d0caa5cef24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320061} {:answer/id #uuid "c025be3d-c1ce-55be-b410-9c2256e83726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320062} {:answer/id #uuid "f744ebb6-34e0-53b4-a486-8331f054d65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320063} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa9ab1b-4f5e-5c9d-ae54-cc56a6f3f5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320064} {:answer/id #uuid "75489219-717c-5d17-aeef-f21f4c739b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75489219-717c-5d17-aeef-f21f4c739b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320065} :answer-field/id #uuid "da282941-a89a-5c51-ac40-7da982297231" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37dcdd00-56a5-5c08-bbf9-c6700f47b443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320067} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c6a511-7ce2-5631-9c23-fe4364e4b85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320068} {:answer/id #uuid "900f6818-48e4-51d5-8c27-8746eb677d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320069} {:answer/id #uuid "dc333ece-532d-54c7-ba2b-2f90d757b1e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320070} {:answer/id #uuid "c7143e6e-e056-566b-b5d1-2a6ee4eff0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320071} {:answer/id #uuid "0b777078-5e33-5f45-86b0-7285724f6a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c6a511-7ce2-5631-9c23-fe4364e4b85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320068} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd718a3c-97c1-5f12-84f4-66ed25d81385" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89dc302-60ce-5bd9-b567-660590ce563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341140" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)=\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-8},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{{e}^{-8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+8},\\,s>-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{s+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+8)-(s-8)}{(s-8)(s+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{16}{{s}^{2}-64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (8,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-8,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(8,\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(8,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eff272b-9d2d-583d-8c07-25b0cbb53a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s+24}{8({s}^{2}-64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320075} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a16aa-f433-5497-b4f7-26543b55ffc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s-24}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320076} {:answer/id #uuid "64c23a6c-0168-551c-b44c-df718ccaa597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+24}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320077} {:answer/id #uuid "0d738e99-37d3-5108-864e-a21560a85d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s+24}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320078} {:answer/id #uuid "f9946be9-db39-5e3b-9e9c-d14212dfd751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s+3}{{s}^{2}-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d738e99-37d3-5108-864e-a21560a85d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s+24}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320078} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9cd507-9a9a-5e73-9663-7ac5f0bd646e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9bfd713-c584-51f5-ac13-9683fe57920b" :question/math-academy-id "q-341190" :question/problem "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ for $s>8.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+L{3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} \\\\  & =4L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+3L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64},\\,s>8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =4L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(8t)}+3L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64}+3\\cdot \\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}-64}+\\frac{24}{{s}^{2}-64} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s+24}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e6f5fa-7d08-5980-a525-5cf8599c28cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+54}{{s}^{2}-81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320082} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf74c35-07f4-505a-8448-3614827256b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18s-54}{{s}^{2}-81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320083} {:answer/id #uuid "1475c026-7370-5671-8d5f-542843681e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s+54}{9({s}^{2}-81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320084} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d08b1c-0cd2-55b0-b431-da033f370f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6s-18}{{s}^{2}-81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320085} {:answer/id #uuid "65a91741-a7b4-5b5e-8016-8a06fd4302c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s+54}{{s}^{2}-81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a91741-a7b4-5b5e-8016-8a06fd4302c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s+54}{{s}^{2}-81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320086} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9e406b-acba-5aa8-92af-42a474bc66e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd36dbb5-36b9-50d8-a3e3-293d0d249fd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341191" :question/problem "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)+6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)$ for $s>9.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)+6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} & =L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+L{6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} \\\\  & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+6L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-81},\\,s>9 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} & =\\frac{9}{{s}^{2}-81},\\,s>9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)}+6L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-81}+6\\cdot \\frac{9}{{s}^{2}-81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-81}+\\frac{54}{{s}^{2}-81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s+54}{{s}^{2}-81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b43f4bf9-b7e5-5835-bfa6-bf8c1e7f1ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-60}{{s}^{2}-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320089} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0aa100-5196-58f0-9db6-48878e31a3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6s+10}{{s}^{2}-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320090} {:answer/id #uuid "15113011-40ae-5dac-ab5d-f5c3ac1dcd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6s+10}{10({s}^{2}-100)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320091} {:answer/id #uuid "3c52c387-2b8a-5096-9259-95fa82781f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+60}{{s}^{2}-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320092} {:answer/id #uuid "b51f65b3-df7d-5d09-b924-e2dcdb0e0de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10s-6}{{s}^{2}-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0aa100-5196-58f0-9db6-48878e31a3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6s+10}{{s}^{2}-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320090} :answer-field/id #uuid "44823150-39ec-5719-98f3-e7e2885fa24d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3db3a05-469c-5a36-89ee-a774cc74a812" :question/math-academy-id "q-341192" :question/problem "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)$ for $s>10.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} & =L{6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} \\\\  & =6L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-100},\\,s>10 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} & =\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100},\\,s>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(10t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(10t)} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-100}+\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100} \\\\  & =\\frac{6s}{{s}^{2}-100}+\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-100} \\\\  & =\\frac{6s+10}{{s}^{2}-100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08466b2-6a52-5d2d-854f-41c691eec74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(s + 4)/((s - 2)(s + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146936} {:answer/id #uuid "0355b8f6-cadd-5a3a-9e2f-02fe0c277166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (6)/((s + 2)(s - 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146937} {:answer/id #uuid "0df6ea69-1bd0-55be-968d-e3dae171da50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2s)/((s + 2)(s - 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146938} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfeca58-6dc8-5979-9085-65e6631ba597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2s - 6)/((s + 2)(s - 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146939} {:answer/id #uuid "507beb4e-935f-5587-ab40-2b033d330c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (2)/((s - 2)(s + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0355b8f6-cadd-5a3a-9e2f-02fe0c277166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (6)/((s + 2)(s - 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146937} :answer-field/id #uuid "08888f2b-a76a-5e08-8194-aec64b32cdda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5620868a-e836-565c-a7cc-7e2d887588f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-89589" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$"}]}
